WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:29.220
Thank you very much. The Cancer Drugs Fund
was set up in 2010 to address some very specific

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:40.859
issues, and that was requests from patient
groups and charities to ensure that patients

00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:50.160
got drugs which were deemed to be useful but
had either received a no from NICE, or were

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:58.929
for rare cancers that were not being assessed
by NICE, or for off-label use. The budget

00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:06.990
was originally £200 million, and it has been
expanded twice and is now £340 million for

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:15.470
the financial year 2015/2016.
The Cancer Drugs Fund is due for renewal in

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:21.540
2016, and there have been several things that
have become clear, but basically the Cancer

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:27.090
Drugs Fund is not really working in the way
that people would like. First of all, there

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:33.920
are issues of cost control. Secondly, the
Cancer Drugs Fund has demonstrably produced

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:41.990
a perverse incentive in terms of enabling
some companies to bypass the rigorous assessments

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:52.640
by NICE and get drugs into the Cancer Drugs
Fund at a higher price. The third area is

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:03.030
that it has not provided the level of evaluation,
I think, that many people would like. Then

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:08.819
the fourth area is that on top of all of this,
there are a whole bunch of new drugs on the

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:13.230
horizon which may be much more curative than
some of the ones we are looking at at the

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:21.860
moment, which may apply to smaller groups
of people and which are more expensive. We

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:28.170
have to address all of these if we are going
to bring the benefit of new drugs to as many

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:34.800
people as possible.
Given that there was a widespread feeling

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:50.530
that the Cancer Drugs Fund could be improved,
I brought together in December 2014 a group

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:59.230
of charities, the MHRA, the Department of
Health, NHS England and others who were interested

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:12.420
in the Cancer Drugs Fund, to see how we could
start to flesh 
out some thoughts as to how we could get a

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.290
sustainable Cancer Drugs Fund. This included
the Association of British Pharmaceutical

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.400
Industry. That was against a background of
quite a lot of moving parts, because the licensing

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:26.630
process in Europe for drugs was changing,
which meant that, with something called adaptive

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:34.400
licensing, some drugs would become available
earlier but with less evidence. The MHRA had

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:42.920
a scheme for early access to medicines, and
we had the Cancer Drugs Fund. On top of that,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:50.020
we had a Cancer Drugs Fund which functioned
outwith the framework of NICE, and of course,

00:03:50.020 --> 00:04:00.550
NICE was established 1999/2000 with a view
of trying to bring rigour to the evidence

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:07.920
and evaluation of clinical effectiveness and
utility of specific drugs.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.430
I think there was a strong view that we needed
to bring all of these moving parts together

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:24.479
in a way that would create at least some form
of functioning, coherent machine. I should

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:33.669
declare an interest in the sense that I have
a very, very strong view of the value of charities;

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:38.680
I am on the Council of the British Heart Foundation,
I am a member of the CRUK company. One thing

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:44.159
that charities bring is they bring independence.
The second thing they bring is credibility

00:04:44.159 --> 00:04:51.830
to their constituents. The third thing they
bring is that they understand what their constituents,

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:57.219
their patients, their members want. The fourth
thing they bring, particularly the big ones,

00:04:57.219 --> 00:05:01.849
is really cutting-edge research, which helps
you to understand the direction of travel

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:09.629
in that particular area. In my mind, the charities
were absolutely key towards helping us find

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:19.699
that sustainable solution.
We brought this group together. We became

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:26.889
concerned about how we could integrate that
work with a new thing called the Accelerated

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:32.139
Access Review, which was a plan by the Department
of Health to see how, across the board, we

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:37.960
could bring earlier treatments to patients
in this country, so it seemed sensible that

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.949
we did not have deliberations on the Cancer
Drugs Fund happening in one place and deliberations

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:49.889
on all other drugs happening elsewhere. Therefore,
last month we paused that work while we spend

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:55.059
a bit of time trying to work out how some
of these moving parts fit together, but it

00:05:55.059 --> 00:06:03.330
is absolutely clear to me that once we understand
some of those issues, we continue to engage

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:11.860
strongly with colleagues in the third sector.
I think the ultimate vision – and I will

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:17.150
simplify this, because otherwise we could
get into quite a technocratic discussion – is

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:27.309
that the Cancer Drugs Fund needs to be an
evaluative incubator, a place where we can

00:06:27.309 --> 00:06:35.279
put drugs and resolve uncertainty about their
effectiveness and utility through data collection;

00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:40.210
that we would, with all drugs in the Cancer
Drugs Fund, collect information that is of

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:46.430
clinical, commercial and regulatory value,
which would enable us to determine fairly

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:56.180
early on whether those drugs are worthy of
funding in our National Health Service. The

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:02.809
second thing is that that incubator function
needs to be linked into NICE, so that we need

00:07:02.809 --> 00:07:09.240
a way of taking drugs where NICE may feel
that there is a degree of uncertainty and

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:15.710
solving that problem with real-world data.
Then the third issue is that we need to be

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:24.460
sure that whatever fund we have fits within
the £300 million budget that we have allocated

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:29.240
for it.
There is some complex work that needs to be

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:36.919
done between now and 2016 to ensure that we
have a working model for the new Cancer Drugs

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:43.979
Fund. That is quite a tight timeline, and
there are some very complex issues to be resolved

00:07:43.979 --> 00:08:03.469
amongst a number of different organisations.
I think you have put the context very clearly.

00:08:03.469 --> 00:08:08.240
The context is that you, Bruce, have been
through a process of informal engagement and

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.169
discussion with various groups, including
charities and the pharmaceutical industry.

00:08:11.169 --> 00:08:19.020
There have been some technical discussions
as to how a new fund approach might be created,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.599
and we are being invited today to approve
the issuance of a consultation paper with

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:29.029
a broader remit for consultation as to the
future operation of the fund.

00:08:29.029 --> 00:08:34.339
We need to go out to public consultation on
the future of the fund, and we need to get

00:08:34.339 --> 00:08:40.759
ourselves into a good position; both in terms
of the technocratic issues of that, as well

00:08:40.759 --> 00:08:47.380
as ensuring that prior to that consultation
we have had good engagement with everybody

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:50.959
who has a vested interest in the Cancer Drugs
Fund.

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:56.630
I might just underline one point, which obviously
is that nothing about changing the way the

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:03.339
fund works per se either increases or cuts
the amount of money in it. So if we are going

00:09:03.339 --> 00:09:08.620
to do what Bruce has described, and which
I think will actually have a strong degree

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:13.759
of support, we may well end up with different
drugs being funded in the Cancer Drugs Fund,

00:09:13.759 --> 00:09:20.769
but it is not going to be an open-ended resource,
so there are still going to be difficult decisions.

00:09:20.769 --> 00:09:27.860
It is possible that, as a result of overcoming
some of the perverse pricing incentives, with

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.300
NICE more heavily engaged, we will be able
to get more value for patients, more patient

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:38.370
access from the money that we are spending,
but I do not think we should be under the

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:42.490
illusion that there are not still going to
be incredibly difficult choices and trade-offs

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:50.810
however the new Cancer Drugs Fund is structured.
Therefore, all the more reason for good stakeholder

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:58.410
engagement. I think a lot of the feeling that
is out there at the moment is that, as the

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:03.009
technocratic thing started to rear its head,
then all of a sudden the other stakeholders

00:10:03.009 --> 00:10:11.779
were left outside the room. Whether it’s
charities, ABPI or anyone else, they want

00:10:11.779 --> 00:10:18.970
some reassurance that the principles that
the working party came up with have not stopped,

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:27.930
and that before any technocratic decisions
are nailed the re-engagement actually happens.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:36.910
Because if we go back to the origins again,
this was around a decision which was made

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:44.370
that, if NICE was not going to adapt its process
to what either patients or politicians were

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:49.089
saying, then there was a workaround put in
place. So there is a bit of suspicion that

00:10:49.089 --> 00:10:54.740
NICE needs to come to the table and everybody
be in the room at the same time, so that whether

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.370
you are a charity or the ABPI, you hear NICE
talking, rather than something happening behind

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:05.579
a locked door which will generate the suspicion.
Thank you for that. There are a couple of

00:11:05.579 --> 00:11:11.620
things. Firstly, I think what became clear
fairly early on in these discussions is that

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:18.310
I think we got to a place where NICE and the
charities were talking in a constructive way.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:23.089
The second thing I would like to say is that
the work that has been done by that working

00:11:23.089 --> 00:11:28.290
group, which included the charities, the ABPI,
NICE, the Department of Health and others,

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:37.180
has been absolutely fundamental in informing
the technocratic discussions. You are absolutely

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:44.230
right; we need to go back to that group now
and engage with them in terms of where those

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:50.920
technocratic solutions are beginning to emerge.
For me, there is a generic message here for

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:55.170
us as NHS England. If we are talking about
a fundamental transformation of the way that

00:11:55.170 --> 00:12:00.610
the National Health Service operates, then
sleek stakeholder management has to be part

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:09.120
of that, and genuine authentic engagement
with people has to be part of that, and not

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.040
leaving people for whatever reason feeling
that they have been left out. It just has

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.750
to be part of who we are, because we keep
saying it is part of who we are, and so we

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:23.480
cannot afford the cognitive dissonance.
I think the point is well made, Ciarán, and

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:29.379
thank you for doing so. As I understand it,
the consultation paper will pick up some of

00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:37.199
the machinery; I do not like this word ‘technocratic.’
It is the machinery as to how you have drugs

00:12:37.199 --> 00:12:42.279
flowing through funding, through the Cancer
Drugs Fund, for the future. It is machinery

00:12:42.279 --> 00:12:48.389
as to how we and NICE are engaged in the technical
evaluation of the drugs, and it is machinery

00:12:48.389 --> 00:12:55.459
as to how we hold down cost. I think that
once these details are clear and the consultation

00:12:55.459 --> 00:13:02.350
paper is issued, then we have the opportunity
for the full engagement with others, but it

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:12.209
is, as far as I am concerned, an open consultation,
but with a timeline; we must get this nailed

00:13:12.209 --> 00:13:19.430
down in advance of 2016/2017.
What I am going to do then is to invite the

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:25.930
Board to do the two things which are mentioned
on the front of the paper, which is to note

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:32.209
the future proposals, but in particular, to
delegate authority to the Chair and the Chief

00:13:32.209 --> 00:13:37.430
Executive. I like this phrase, ‘We may act
jointly and/or individually, where either

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:44.389
of them is unavailable.’ We will both be
available, thank you very much, but we will

00:13:44.389 --> 00:13:51.069
of course consider the consultation paper
and approve it for taking out for consultation.

00:13:51.069 --> 00:13:55.550
Are we all content with that? Good. Thank
you very much indeed.

